  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between lidocaine and the CD70 antigen through a series of molecular interactions. Lidocaine is identified as having a target of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which is itself a target of multiple compounds, including (2R,3R,4S,5R)-2-(6-aminopurin-9-yl)-5-methanidyl-tetrahydrofuran-3,4-diol and 7,10-Octadecadienoic acid, methyl ester. These compounds are ultimately linked to the CD70 antigen, indicating a possible pathway through which lidocaine may influence the expression or activity of CD70. The scores from the three paths are consistent, with a final score of 0.5219 in two of them, suggesting a moderate level of confidence in the relationship. The evidence highlights the role of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex in mediating this interaction, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The presence of multiple compounds targeting the same complex supports the idea of a broader biological context, but further research is needed to confirm the direct involvement of lidocaine in CD70 regulation.",
